en-US.description:
Aviation fans around the world are buzzing with excitement over the news that the U.S. Navy’s legendary F-14 Tomcat may fly in American skies again. This "legendary cat," the star of the movie *Top Gun* and unseen since its retirement in 2006, is preparing for a revival after 20 years.

Recently, the so-called "Maverick Act" was passed in the U.S. Congress. The core of this bill is the transfer of three retired F-14D aircraft, currently stored at the "Boneyard" in Arizona, to the U.S. Space Rocket Center in Alabama. What is surprising is that they are not simply being transferred for display purposes. The bill explicitly states that the Navy will provide spare parts and manuals to restore one of these aircraft to "flight-ready condition."

The sole reason the U.S. military has been completely dismantling retired Tomcats was to prevent parts from being leaked to Iran, the sole operator. However, as it was determined that the Tomcat fleet in Iran had been effectively neutralized due to recent changes in the international situation and airstrikes, the U.S. Congress finally made the decision to preserve this aviation heritage. In effect, the security barriers have been lifted for the first time in 20 years.

Of course, there are many hurdles to overcome before they can fly again. The structural safety of the aircraft, which has been dormant for decades exposed to desert winds, must be re-evaluated from scratch, and massive costs and specialized personnel must be invested to revive the Tomcat's unique and complex variable-geometry wing system. Maintenance costs are also astronomical, with the cost of a single full tank of fuel amounting to approximately 20 million Korean won.

However, for those who cherish the nostalgia of 'Top Gun,' hearing the sound of the Tomcat's engines again is a priceless dream. The entire world is holding its breath, waiting for this bill, which has passed the Senate, to be finalized so that we can once again witness the Tomcat taking off spectacularly at airshows. Video Source: DVIDS

